OBM Shipping charters crude and product tankers for companies that load or discharge in Türkiye, and for anyone moving oil through the Bosphorus and the Dardanelles. Our tanker desk is in Istanbul, so Black Sea and Mediterranean fixtures are worked in the same time zone as the cargo, the Straits and most of the counterparties. Send the cargo, ports and laycan, and a broker replies within 60 minutes during business hours.

## What does the Istanbul tanker desk fix?

- **Crude oil** on [VLCC](/tankers/vlcc), [Suezmax](/tankers/suezmax) and [Aframax](/tankers/aframax) tankers.
- **Clean products** (gasoline, diesel, jet fuel and naphtha) on LR2, [LR1](/tankers/lr1) and [MR](/tankers/mr) tankers.
- **Spot voyages, time charters and contracts of affreightment (COA)**, and the post-fixture work that follows: laytime, demurrage and claims.

We work for charterers (refiners, traders, importers and end users) and for owners and operators looking for cargo. On every fixture we make clear which side we represent.

## Why fix Turkish and Black Sea cargoes through an Istanbul broker?

Most crude and product cargoes leaving the Black Sea pass through the Turkish Straits, and the days a ship spends waiting there often decide whether a voyage makes money. Being on the Bosphorus, we follow transit conditions and the regional owners and charterers every day. Every Black Sea estimate we send states the waiting days we have assumed for that ship size, direction and season, with pilotage, tugs and Montreux dues as separate lines. The [Turkish Straits guide](/turkish-straits) explains how transits work.

Turkish trades have their own rhythm as well: refinery and terminal calls in İzmit Bay, Aliağa and the Gulf of İskenderun, crude exports from Ceyhan, and product imports into Marmara and Mediterranean terminals. Our [guide to Turkish ports](/guides/turkish-ports-for-tankers-and-lpg) covers the authorities, cabotage and the terminal checks to make before fixing.

## Do you also cover long-haul cargoes into Türkiye?

Yes. Crude and products for Turkish refineries and terminals come from the Black Sea and the Mediterranean, and from further afield. Our London brokers cover the Atlantic basin and our Singapore brokers cover East of Suez, so a long-haul cargo is worked across time zones by the same team. Middle East Gulf loadings have been severely disrupted since March 2026; our [Strait of Hormuz checklist](/blog/strait-of-hormuz-disruption-checklist-for-tanker-and-lpg-charterers) sets out what that means for a fixture.

## What should you send for a firm answer?

{% callout label="The order" linkHref="/contact?segment=crude" linkLabel="Send an inquiry" %}
Cargo grade, quantity and tolerance; load and discharge terminals (or ranges); laycan; voyage, time charter or COA; your charter party form and riders; ship requirements (age, approvals, coating or heating); and the counterparties' legal names for compliance screening. For Russian-origin oil, the price-cap attestation chain.
{% /callout %}

With that, we come back with suitable ships, a freight idea and the assumptions behind it. The [step-by-step chartering guide](/guides/how-to-charter-a-tanker) explains what happens next.

## How do we quote and compare offers?

Spot crude and product voyages are usually quoted in Worldscale points or as a lump sum. We show the time-charter equivalent (TCE) with every freight idea, so ships in different positions and on different terms can be compared on one number; the [Worldscale and TCE guide](/blog/worldscale-and-tce-explained-how-tanker-freight-is-quoted) explains the method. To run a first estimate yourself, the [freight calculator](/freight-calculator) covers ballast and laden legs, Turkish Straits passages, bunkers, port costs, commissions and EU ETS, using dated reference prices.

## Which checks come before an offer?

Counterparty screening, ship screening and, for Russian-origin cargoes, price-cap attestation checks run before a freight idea goes back to the charterer. We do not work inquiries that fail compliance review. Our [compliance page](/compliance) sets out the four layers we check.

## What happens after the fixture?

The work does not stop when the ship is fixed. We follow every stage from voyage orders and notice of readiness to the laytime calculation and the demurrage claim. A demurrage claim not presented with its documents within the charter party's time bar is lost, so we collect the documents during the voyage. Our [laytime and demurrage guide](/blog/laytime-and-demurrage-a-practical-guide-for-tanker-and-lpg-charters) shows a worked example.

## Who pays the broker?

Brokerage is normally paid by the shipowner as a percentage of the freight or hire, as set out in the charter party. Charterers often receive an address commission as well. Sending us an inquiry costs nothing.

